    "priority_2_discover_click_and_stay": {
      "statement": "This post exists to do exactly two things in sequence: (1) earn the click in the Discover feed off the title + hero image alone, and (2) earn the stay once clicked — real dwell time, a reader who reads to the end. Nothing else in this prompt matters if either of these fails. This is not an SEO/AEO answer-engine document like the blog prompt — Discover ranking is entity- and interest-matched, not keyword-matched, and it runs on a feedback loop: strong early engagement gets the post pushed to a wider audience, weak engagement (clicks but fast bounce) gets it killed within hours.",
      "click_mechanics": [
        "The title and the hero image are the ENTIRE pitch. The reader sees nothing else before deciding to tap. Every other writing decision is downstream of getting these two right.",
        "Title formula: negative angle + solution, or a genuine curiosity gap, or FOMO/trend urgency — see hook_and_emotion_rules below. Must be specific and verifiable, never vague or sensationalised. A title that overpromises and underdelivers gets actively suppressed by Google's classifier, not just ignored.",
        "Hero image is not a decorative afterthought — it is co-equal with the title as the click driver. See hero_image_creative_brief below."
      ],
      "stay_mechanics": [
        "First paragraph must deliver immediately — no throat-clearing, no runway. The reader decided to click on an emotional promise; the first few sentences either pay it off or the reader bounces.",
        "Dwell time and 'read to the end' behaviour is a direct input to whether Discover keeps showing this post to more people. Every section must earn the scroll to the next section.",
        "This is why word count is fixed at 1500 with real depth per section, not padded — a reader who bounces at word 400 of a padded 1500 words kills the post's reach faster than a tight 1500 that holds attention throughout."
      ]
    },
    "priority_3_zero_manual_edit_standard": {
      "statement": "The output must not require a human pass to de-AI-ify it. There is no separate 'human value' input field in this version of the system — the standard is that the AI-written post itself must not read as generic, templated, or AI-produced anywhere in it. This is a live, continuous self-check during writing, not a post-write cleanup pass.",
      "live_self_check_rule": "HARD RULE: while writing each paragraph, actively ask — does this sentence, this transition, this H2 opening sound like something any AI would produce for any topic, or does it sound like it could only have been written by someone who actually knows nail trends and actually cares about this specific reader right now? If a sentence could be dropped into a different Nail Feed post on a different topic with only the nouns changed, it is too generic — rewrite it with something specific to THIS topic, THIS trend moment, THIS reader.",
      "why_this_is_load_bearing": "The founder's own review process has explicitly named this the standard: read the finished post, and if any H2 or paragraph reads as generic AI output, it should not need manual editing to fix. That bar is set here, in the writing pass, not as a downstream QA step."
